        <dd class="mt-3 font-sans text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-dim">Each deployment has one ordered commit log, which keeps authorization, audit, and pruning in one place. There is no peer-to-peer mode.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-void p-6 sm:p-7">
        <dt class="text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.06em]">Your app merges</dt>
        <dd class="mt-3 font-sans text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-dim">Rows converge by version. Your code decides what a conflict resolves to; Syncular delivers both sides.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-void p-6 sm:p-7">
        <dt class="text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.06em]">CRDT per column</dt>
        <dd class="mt-3 font-sans text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-dim">Collaborative text uses <a href="/concepts-crdt/" class="ul">CRDT columns</a> (Yjs on the web, yrs in Rust). Every other column is a versioned value.</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-void p-6 sm:p-7">
        <dt class="text-[13px] font-semibold tracking-[0.06em]">Durable app data</dt>
        <dd class="mt-3 font-sans text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-dim">Syncular stores authorized records. Frame-by-frame multiplayer state belongs in a dedicated netcode layer.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
